# @cover src/app/dashboard.render/adapter.spl 60%
# @cover src/app/office/render_adapter.spl 40%
"""
## Purpose and audience
Purpose: produces non-empty text output
Audience: compiler and tooling engineers who maintain this spec

# UI Render Integration Specification

**Feature IDs:** #UI-RENDER-002
**Category:** Tooling
**Difficulty:** 2/5
**Status:** Implemented
**Requirements:** N/A
**Plan:** N/A
**Design:** doc/05_design/ui_render_feature_caret.md
**Research:** N/A

## Overview

Integration tests for the render adapters. Validates that the dashboard
adapter and office adapter produce meaningful output through the shared
RenderResult contract, and that unknown adapter names are handled gracefully.

## Key Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| DashboardRenderRequest | Dashboard-specific render request with summary dict |
| render_dashboard | Dashboard adapter entry point producing RenderResult |
| office_render | Office adapter entry point routing to sub-apps |
| Adapter routing | Config.adapter_name selects which sub-app handles the request |

## Behavior

- Dashboard adapter produces text output containing status information
- Dashboard adapter produces HTML output containing styled cards
- Office adapter routes to word, sheets, slides, mail, planner, launcher
- Unknown adapter_name in office adapter returns warning message
"""

# @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001

use std.spipe.*
use app.ui.render.types.{RenderConfig, RenderResult}
use app.dashboard.render.adapter.{render_dashboard, DashboardRenderRequest,
    default_dashboard_config}
use app.office.render_adapter.{office_render}


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 1: Dashboard Adapter
# ============================================================================

use std.spec.step
describe "Dashboard Render Adapter":
    """
    ## Dashboard rendering through shared contract

    The dashboard adapter takes a DashboardRenderRequest (config + summary dict)
    and produces a RenderResult with text and/or HTML output depending on format.
    """

    context "text output for status mode":
        """
        ### Scenario: Default status mode with text format

        The dashboard should produce a non-empty text output containing
        project status information when given a summary dict.
        """

        it "produces non-empty text output":
            step("Verify: produces non-empty text output")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            val cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            val len = result.text_output.len()
            expect(len > 0).to_equal(true)

        it "text output contains status header":
            step("Verify: text output contains status header")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            val cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("Status")

        it "text output contains feature count":
            step("Verify: text output contains feature count")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            val cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("10")

        it "sets metadata adapter to dashboard":
            step("Verify: sets metadata adapter to dashboard")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            val cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            expect(result.metadata["adapter"]).to_equal("dashboard")

    context "html output":
        """
        ### Scenario: HTML format rendering

        When format is "html", the dashboard should produce HTML output
        containing styled elements.
        """

        it "produces non-empty html output when format is html":
            step("Verify: produces non-empty html output when format is html")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            var cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            cfg.format = "html"
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            val len = result.html_output.len()
            expect(len > 0).to_equal(true)

        it "html output contains DOCTYPE":
            step("Verify: html output contains DOCTYPE")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            var cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            cfg.format = "html"
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            expect(result.html_output).to_contain("DOCTYPE")

        it "html output contains dashboard heading":
            step("Verify: html output contains dashboard heading")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            var cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            cfg.format = "html"
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            expect(result.html_output).to_contain("Dashboard")

    context "both format":
        """
        ### Scenario: Dual output with both format

        When format is "both", the dashboard should produce both text and HTML.
        """

        it "produces both text and html output":
            step("Verify: produces both text and html output")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            var cfg = default_dashboard_config()
            cfg.format = "both"
            val summary = build_test_summary()
            val req = DashboardRenderRequest(config: cfg, summary: summary)
            val result = render_dashboard(req)
            val text_len = result.text_output.len()
            val html_len = result.html_output.len()
            expect(text_len > 0).to_equal(true)
            expect(html_len > 0).to_equal(true)


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 2: Office Adapter Routing
# ============================================================================

describe "Office Render Adapter Routing":
    """
    ## Office adapter_name routing

    The office adapter uses config.adapter_name to route to the
    correct sub-app renderer (word, sheets, slides, mail, planner, launcher).
    Unknown names should return a result with a warning.
    """

    context "unknown adapter_name":
        """
        ### Scenario: Unrecognized adapter name

        When adapter_name is not a known office sub-app, the adapter
        should return a result with a warning message.
        """

        it "returns text mentioning unknown adapter":
            step("Verify: returns text mentioning unknown adapter")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = "nonexistent_app"

            val result = office_render(cfg)
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("Unknown")

        it "includes warning for unknown adapter":
            step("Verify: includes warning for unknown adapter")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = "nonexistent_app"

            val result = office_render(cfg)
            val has_warnings = result.warnings.len() > 0
            expect(has_warnings).to_equal(true)

    context "known adapter names route correctly":
        """
        ### Scenario: Valid adapter names

        Each recognized adapter_name should be accepted without errors.
        We test with "word" as a representative sub-app.
        """

        it "word adapter produces non-empty text output":
            step("Verify: word adapter produces non-empty text output")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = "word"

            val result = office_render(cfg)
            val len = result.text_output.len()
            expect(len > 0).to_equal(true)

        it "word adapter text output contains app name":
            step("Verify: word adapter text output contains app name")
            # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
            # Naming convention (decided 2026-08-17, see
            # office_render_adapter_unknown_name_and_naming_drift_2026-07-20.md
            # Finding 2): the LibreOffice-branded name is the canonical app
            # display name suite-wide; "word" is only the internal routing key,
            # which text_output already carries as the "(word, ...)" tag.
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = "word"

            val result = office_render(cfg)
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("Writer")
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("(word,")
            expect(result.warnings.len()).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract


describe "office_render adapter-name validation (defect class)":
    # Class: an adapter name that _normalize_surface does not recognise used to
    # be relabelled "suite" and rendered silently, so a caller typo was
    # indistinguishable from a deliberate suite-index request. Every accepted
    # alias must stay silent; every unrecognised name must be reported.
    it "every documented alias routes without a warning":
        step("Verify: every documented alias routes without a warning")
        # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
        val aliases = ["word", "writer", "calc", "excel", "sheets", "impress",
            "ppt", "slides", "mail", "planner", "draw", "base", "db", "math"]
        for name in aliases:
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = name
            val result = office_render(cfg)
            expect(result.warnings.len()).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("Office render:")
            expect(result.text_output.contains("Unknown")).to_equal(false)
            expect(result.html_output.len() > 0).to_equal(true)

    it "every unrecognised name is reported, not silently accepted":
        step("Verify: every unrecognised name is reported, not silently accepted")
        # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
        val bogus = ["nonexistent_app", "wrod", "spreadsheet", "powerpoint", "zzz"]
        for name in bogus:
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = name
            val result = office_render(cfg)
            expect(result.warnings.len() > 0).to_equal(true)
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("Unknown")
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain(name)
            # still renders the suite index rather than failing outright
            expect(result.html_output.len() > 0).to_equal(true)

    it "an empty adapter name is a deliberate suite-index request, not an error":
        step("Verify: an empty adapter name is a deliberate suite-index request, not an error")
        # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
        var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
        cfg.adapter_name = ""
        val result = office_render(cfg)
        expect(result.warnings.len()).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(result.text_output.contains("Unknown")).to_equal(false)
        expect(result.html_output.len() > 0).to_equal(true)

    it "alias casing does not change whether a name is accepted":
        step("Verify: alias casing does not change whether a name is accepted")
        # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
        for name in ["WORD", "Writer", "CaLc"]:
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = name
            val result = office_render(cfg)
            expect(result.warnings.len()).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
            expect(result.text_output.contains("Unknown")).to_equal(false)


describe "office_render display-name convention (defect class)":
    # Class: display-name drift. The suite is LibreOffice-branded
    # (src/app/office/libreoffice.spl is the single branding source, and
    # `_title_for` builds every title as "{libreoffice_suite_name()} <App>").
    # The Office-style words word/excel/ppt are internal ROUTING KEYS only.
    # A drift on ANY one surface — not just the "word" one that was reported
    # in office_render_adapter_unknown_name_and_naming_drift_2026-07-20.md —
    # is a user-visible naming bug, so every surface is checked here.
    it "every surface titles itself with its LibreOffice application name":
        step("Verify: every surface titles itself with its LibreOffice application name")
        # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
        val pairs = [["word", "Writer"], ["writer", "Writer"],
            ["sheets", "Calc"], ["calc", "Calc"], ["excel", "Calc"],
            ["slides", "Impress"], ["impress", "Impress"], ["ppt", "Impress"],
            ["mail", "Mail"], ["planner", "Planner"], ["draw", "Draw"],
            ["base", "Base"], ["db", "Base"], ["math", "Math"]]
        for pair in pairs:
            val alias = pair[0]
            val app_name = pair[1]
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = alias
            val result = office_render(cfg)
            expect(result.text_output).to_contain("LibreOffice {app_name}")

    it "no surface leaks a foreign-suite brand name into its title":
        step("Verify: no surface leaks a foreign-suite brand name into its title")
        # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
        val aliases = ["word", "writer", "calc", "excel", "sheets", "impress",
            "ppt", "slides", "mail", "planner", "draw", "base", "db", "math", ""]
        for name in aliases:
            var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
            cfg.adapter_name = name
            val result = office_render(cfg)
            expect(result.text_output.contains("Microsoft")).to_equal(false)
            expect(result.text_output.contains("Word")).to_equal(false)
            expect(result.text_output.contains("Excel")).to_equal(false)
            expect(result.text_output.contains("PowerPoint")).to_equal(false)

    it "the suite index itself uses the branded suite name":
        step("Verify: the suite index itself uses the branded suite name")
        # @req: REQ-APP-RendInte-001
        var cfg = RenderConfig.default()
        cfg.adapter_name = ""
        val result = office_render(cfg)
        expect(result.text_output).to_contain("LibreOffice Suite")


# ============================================================================
# Helper Code
# ============================================================================

fn build_test_summary() -> Dict<text, text>:
    """Build a minimal test summary dict for dashboard rendering."""
    var d: Dict<text, text> = {}
    d["features_total"] = "10"
    d["features_complete"] = "5"
    d["features_in_progress"] = "3"
    d["features_planned"] = "2"
    d["todos_total"] = "20"
    d["todos_open"] = "15"
    d["todos_blocked"] = "2"
    d["tests_total"] = "100"
    d["tests_passed"] = "90"
    d["tests_failed"] = "5"
    d["tests_skipped"] = "5"
    d["lines_total"] = "10000"
    d["lines_covered"] = "7500"
    d["coverage_pct"] = "75"
    return d
